course: dictionary
lesson: 12873
title: Enroll: signing up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To enroll means to put your name on the list to join something, like a school, a course, a club or a class. Someone who does the signing up is enrolling. «Mum will enroll me in the swimming club on Monday.» Mum puts my name on the list, so I can join the club.
explain: The word is spelled enroll in American English and enrol in British English. They mean exactly the same thing, so do not be confused if you see both. «Ella enrolled at the music school in London.» The -ed ending shows that it happened in the past.
explain: Use the words around enroll to find its meaning. School, club, class and list are clues that it is about joining something. Then try your meaning in the sentence: if «I will sign up for the chess club» still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: To enroll means to put your name on a list to join a school, course, club or class.
fact: To enroll someone means to sign them up.
fact: The US spelling is enroll, enrolls, enrolled and enrolling; the UK spelling is enrol, enrols, enrolled and enrolling.
fact: Common phrases are enroll in, enroll at and enroll for, as in «enroll in an after-school club».
fact: In maths problems, numbers of enrolled pupils can be added, compared or split into groups.
exercise: «Mum will enroll me in the swimming club on Monday.» What does enroll mean here? | To buy a new swimsuit | *To sign me up to join the club | To swim faster than everyone else | To leave the club for good
exercise: «Over sixty pupils enrolled in the new science club this term.» What does enrolled mean? | Ran out of the club | Lost their way home | *Signed up to join | Painted the club walls
exercise: «Twelve pupils enrolled in drama and fifteen pupils enrolled in chess. How many pupils enrolled in all?» | 3 | 30 | 25 | *27
test: «Ella enrolled at the music school in London.» Which ending shows it happened in the past? | -ly | -ing | -s | *-ed
test: Which spelling is the UK spelling of enroll? | enrowl | enrole | enrool | *enrol
exam: «Twenty-four pupils want to enroll in the art club, but only eighteen places are left.» How many pupils are left over? | 42 | 18 | *6 | 4
